    <p>To compute precise withholding values across any invoice or contract value, access our interactive <a href="/countries/india/tds/tds-calculator/" class="text-link font-semibold hover:underline dark:text-link">India TDS Calculator</a>.</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">Section 194J Deconstructed: The Critical 2% vs 10% Division</h2>
    <p><strong>Section 194J differentiates between technical services (FTS taxed at 2%) and professional services (taxed at 10%), a distinction that historically sparked intense litigation between taxpayers and assessing officers.</strong></p>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-6">What Qualifies for the Reduced 2% Rate?</h3>
    <p>Under the amended provisions of Section 194J(1), the following payment streams are taxed at the concessional rate of <strong>2%</strong>:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-1 mt-2 text-sm">
      <li><strong>Fees for Technical Services (FTS):</strong> Any consideration for managerial, technical, or consultancy services (such as IT network maintenance, software coding, routine engineering, or database management).</li>
      <li><strong>Royalty:</strong> Consideration for the sale, distribution, or exhibition of cinematographic films.</li>
      <li><strong>BPO / Call Center Operations:</strong> Income derived by business process outsourcing centers and call center services.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-4">What Remains Taxable at the Higher 10% Rate?</h3>
    <p>Payments for professional services defined under Section 44AA remain subject to <strong>10% TDS</strong>. This includes services rendered by individuals carrying on legal, medical, engineering, architectural, accountancy, technical consultancy, interior decoration, authorized representation, or notified professions (such as film artists, company secretaries, and information technology professionals acting in an individual professional capacity).</p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">Section 194C Contractor Payments: Single vs Aggregate Thresholds</h2>
    <p><strong>Section 194C applies to payments made to contractors or sub-contractors for carrying out any work (including labor supply, advertising, broadcasting, catering, and transport carriage), requiring tax deduction at 1% for individuals/HUFs and 2% for other entities.</strong></p>

    <p>The application of TDS under Section 194C is governed by a dual-threshold safety net:</p>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1.5 mt-2 text-sm">
      <li><strong>Single Bill Threshold:</strong> If a single contract invoice exceeds <strong>₹30,000</strong>, TDS must be deducted on that invoice immediately.</li>
      <li><strong>Aggregate Annual Threshold:</strong> Even if every individual invoice is below ₹30,000 (e.g., ten separate invoices of ₹15,000 each), the moment cumulative payments to that contractor surpass <strong>₹1,00,000</strong> in a single financial year, TDS must be deducted on the invoice that causes the total to cross ₹1,00,000 and all subsequent invoices.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">Filing Quarterly TDS Returns: Forms, Deadlines & TRACES Portal</h2>
    <p><strong>Every deductor must deposit deducted taxes with the central government via Challan ITNS 281 by the 7th of the following month (and April 30 for March deductions), followed by filing quarterly TDS returns on the TRACES portal.</strong></p>

    <div class="my-6 overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-hairline bg-canvas dark:border-overlay-lighter dark:bg-surface">
      <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
        <thead class="bg-canvas-soft border-b border-hairline dark:bg-surface/80 dark:border-overlay-lighter text-ink dark:text-on-primary font-bold">
          <tr>
            <th class="p-3">Quarter</th>
            <th class="p-3">Period Covered</th>
            <th class="p-3">Return Due Date (Forms 24Q, 26Q, 27Q)</th>
            <th class="p-3">Form 16 / 16A Issuance Due Date</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="divide-y divide-hairline dark:divide-overlay-lighter">
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Q1</td><td class="p-3">April 1 – June 30</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">July 31</td><td class="p-3">August 15</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Q2</td><td class="p-3">July 1 – September 30</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">October 31</td><td class="p-3">November 15</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Q3</td><td class="p-3">October 1 – December 31</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">January 31</td><td class="p-3">February 15</td></tr>
          <tr class="bg-canvas-soft"><td class="p-3 font-semibold">Q4</td><td class="p-3">January 1 – March 31</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-primary dark:text-on-primary">May 31</td><td class="p-3">June 15</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p class="text-sm">
      <strong>Primary Return Types:</strong> Form 24Q is filed for salary payments (generating Form 16 for employees); Form 26Q is filed for all domestic non-salary payments (generating Form 16A for vendors); and Form 27Q is filed for payments made to non-resident Indians (NRIs) and foreign corporations.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-ink dark:text-on-primary mt-8">Penalties for Default: Interest, Section 234E & Expense Disallowance</h2>
    <p><strong>Failure to comply with statutory TDS timelines triggers severe compounding consequences under the Income-tax Act, including statutory interest under Section 201(1A), a daily late-filing fee under Section 234E, and a 30% expenditure disallowance under Section 40(a)(ia).</strong></p>

    <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 mt-2 text-sm">
      <li><strong>Interest for Non-Deduction (1% per month):</strong> Calculated from the date the tax was deductible until the date it was actually deducted.</li>
      <li><strong>Interest for Non-Payment (1.5% per month):</strong> Calculated from the date of deduction to the actual date of deposit into the government treasury.</li>
      <li><strong>Late Filing Fee (Section 234E):</strong> A mandatory fee of <strong>₹200 per day</strong> for every day the quarterly return remains unfiled, capped at the total amount of TDS deductible in that return.</li>
      <li><strong>30% Business Expense Disallowance (Section 40(a)(ia)):</strong> If a business fails to deduct TDS or fails to deposit deducted tax before the ITR filing due date, <strong>30% of that entire contract/rent/professional expense is disallowed</strong> from its profit and loss statement, directly inflating taxable business profits.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

